adapter bynk.crypto {
	exports capability { Crypto }
	exports transparent { Digest, Signature, CryptoError }
	exports opaque { SigningKey, VerifyKey }

	---
	A hash function for `Crypto.digest`, `Crypto.hmac` and `Crypto.verifyHmac`.
	The set is what every platform's Web Crypto supports. It may gain
	members in a minor release, so a `match` over it needs a wildcard.
	---
	type Digest = enum { Sha256, Sha384, Sha512 }

	---
	A signature algorithm, fixed when a key is imported. `Ed25519` takes the
	key formats Web Crypto does for it (PKCS#8 to sign, SPKI or 32 raw bytes
	to verify); `EcdsaP256Sha256` is ECDSA over P-256 with SHA-256 and signs
	and verifies the raw (IEEE P1363, `r || s`) form; `RsaPkcs1Sha256` is
	RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 with SHA-256. It may gain members in a minor release,
	so a `match` over it needs a wildcard.
	---
	type Signature = enum { Ed25519, EcdsaP256Sha256, RsaPkcs1Sha256 }

	---
	Why a key import failed. Neither variant carries key material: `reason`
	and `name` describe the failure, never the bytes. `InvalidKey` is a key
	the platform could not parse for the algorithm; `UnsupportedAlgorithm`
	is an algorithm this platform's Web Crypto does not provide (an older
	browser without Ed25519, say), named so the failure is never silent. It
	may gain variants in a minor release, so a `match` over it needs a
	wildcard.
	---
	type CryptoError =
		| InvalidKey(reason: String)
		| UnsupportedAlgorithm(name: String)

	---
	A key that signs, imported by `Crypto.importSigningKey`. It is opaque: its
	bytes cannot be read back, and the platform holds it non-extractable. Like
	a `Stream`, it lives for the call that imported it. It cannot be stored,
	persisted, logged or sent, so it is refused in a `store`, a record or event
	field, a handler parameter or result, and a wire type
	(`bynk.types.key_at_boundary`). Import it again where it is needed.
	---
	type SigningKey = opaque Bytes

	---
	A key that verifies, imported by `Crypto.importVerifyKey` or
	`Crypto.importVerifyJwk`. Opaque and unstorable exactly as `SigningKey` is.
	---
	type VerifyKey = opaque Bytes

	---
	Cryptography over Web Crypto (`crypto.subtle`): digests, HMAC, constant-time
	comparison, and signing and verification with imported keys. Every operation
	is an `Effect` because `crypto.subtle` is asynchronous on every platform, so
	there is no hashing in pure functions, refinements or invariants. Encodings
	(`Bytes.toHex`, `Bytes.fromBase64`, …) stay pure.

	No third-party crypto runs and nothing is polyfilled: a platform without
	`crypto.subtle` (a browser outside a secure context) fails loudly.

	Timing. `verifyHmac`, `verify` and `equal` do not return early on the first
	differing byte, so they are the only way to compare a secret, a MAC, a
	signature or a token's digest; `Bytes.compare` is not constant-time and must
	never compare secrets. `digest`, `hmac` and `sign` run in time that does not
	depend on the data in a way a program can exploit.
	---
	capability Crypto {
		fn digest(algorithm: Digest, data: Bytes) -> Effect[Bytes]
		fn hmac(algorithm: Digest, key: Bytes, data: Bytes) -> Effect[Bytes]
		fn verifyHmac(algorithm: Digest, key: Bytes, data: Bytes, mac: Bytes) -> Effect[Bool]
		fn equal(a: Bytes, b: Bytes) -> Effect[Bool]
		fn importSigningKey(
			algorithm: Signature,
			pkcs8: Bytes
		) -> Effect[Result[SigningKey, CryptoError]]
		fn importVerifyKey(
			algorithm: Signature,
			key: Bytes
		) -> Effect[Result[VerifyKey, CryptoError]]
		fn importVerifyJwk(jwk: String) -> Effect[Result[VerifyKey, CryptoError]]
		fn sign(key: SigningKey, data: Bytes) -> Effect[Bytes]
		fn verify(key: VerifyKey, data: Bytes, signature: Bytes) -> Effect[Bool]
	}

	provides Crypto = CryptoProvider
}
